David Damerell wrote:
> 
> [Fire and cold resistance mutually exclusive]
>
> ...yes, it increases late-game difficulty, but it does so in
> an arbitary fashion that's not consistent with the rest of the game.

	I would like to increase late-game difficulty, so we're
on opposite sides.  I also strongly prefer consistency, so we're
on the same side.  Which half of my personality would you like to
talk to?

	Where do you see it being inconsistent with the rest of
the game?  Is it just that you're used to intrinsics being permanent?  

	For myself, I'm always worried about making the game
mechanics clear (or discoverable) by unspoiled players.  Here, "if
heat, then not cold" should be intuitive enough for people to figure
it out fairly soon.  Unfortunately, this is not true of "Gehennom 
fire traps lower your max HP by 5 if not fire resistant".  For that
reason, I think that the YANI is fine for spoiled players, but can't
be added to the official Nethack without changes.

	Let's think of this as a first try at revising some of the
resistances.  A minimum fix for the unspoiled players would be to
not have fire traps lower max HPs.  Or to advertise the evils of 
fire traps in a fortune and maybe an oracularity.  

	Next, when I first came up with the YANI, I considered 
having air and earth mutually exclusive.  Unfortunately, I didn't
see an *intuitively obvious* way of relating air and earth to any
pairs of resistances.  Yes, you can wedge things in, but only in
rather awkward ways.

	How's this as a second try:  you can be *aligned with* one 
of earth, air, fire, or water.  The element you're aligned with is
set (or reset) by eating the right monsters.  This is separate from
resistances.  (Rename "fire resistance" to "heat resistance".)  Being
aligned with a particular element affects several things:

   o  Monsters.  Unintelligent monsters of your element tend to be
      peaceful.

   o  Resistances.  (Earth:  disintegration.  Air:  none.  Fire:  
      heat.  Water:  acid).  

   o  At this point, to fold in the first version, we might want to
      have *vulnerabilities*.  If you have cold resistance as an 
      intrinsic, but your element is fire, the intrinsic doesn't
      help.

   o  Detection.  You detect things of your element within, say,
      four squares.  All:  monsters of your element.  Earth:  walls;
      rocks; boulders; rock traps.  Air:  corridors and open floor;
      trap doors; pits and holes.  Fire:  lava; fire traps.  Water:
      fountains; water squares; rust traps; potions.  
      
   o  Reactions to traps and terrain.  Earth:  boulders and rocks
      don't hurt you.  Air:  none.  Fire:  fire traps don't hurt
      you.  (Maybe have "drink deeply of the fire?" for the effect
      of an uncursed healing potion while destroying the trap.)
      Water:  water squares are shown as water, but count as open
      terrain.  You can see everything in the water, monsters and
      and objects both.  You can walk across water squares.  You
      don't get any messages like "the water closes above your
      head, but you don't drown!"; the game gives no indication
      that you can distinguish between water and air.

	At this point, if I thought that this idea was workable,
I would go through the lists of traps, terrains, objects... and
pick out which ones fit under this scheme.  As it is, I think that
this counts as YA half-baked NI.  

	It would add a nice bit of complexity to the game, though.


